A rainbow has bands of color because of the way light interacts with water droplets in the air. When sunlight enters a water droplet, it slows down and bends, a process called refraction. This bending causes the light to spread out into different colors, each bending at a slightly different angle. The colors then reflect off the inside of the droplet and exit, creating the visible spectrum of colors we see in a rainbow.
A rainbow always appears on the side away from the sun because the light from the sun is refracted, reflected, and dispersed within the water droplets. The observer must be positioned such that the light is bent and spread out in a way that creates the spectrum of colors. Since the sun is a bright source of light, the observer naturally faces away from it to see the rainbow.
Yes, two rainbows can appear at once, known as a double rainbow. The second rainbow is formed when light is refracted, reflected twice inside the water droplets, and dispersed again, creating a secondary, often fainter arc with reversed color order.
A rainbow is made when sunlight shines on water droplets, like after a rain. The light bends and splits into different colors, making the rainbow. You can see the rainbow on the side opposite the sun because the light is going through the water droplets.
