The story of a fox who learns to swim across a river begins in a lush forest where the river was a formidable barrier. The fox, named Felix, often watched the other animals cross the river with ease, but he was afraid of the water. One day, a wise old heron told Felix that the river was not as dangerous as it seemed and that he could learn to swim.  Felix decided to take the heron's advice and started practicing. He first waded into the river, trying to keep his balance. The water was cool and refreshing, but it was also strong and could easily sweep him away. He struggled for a while, but he didn't give up.  Every day, Felix returned to the river, determined to master the skill of swimming. He practiced his strokes, learned to breathe properly, and even tried to float. With each day, he felt more confident and less afraid of the water.  One day, Felix decided to try swimming across the river. He took a deep breath and jumped in. The water was cold, but he pushed himself forward, using his legs to kick and his arms to stroke. He felt the current pulling him back, but he kept moving forward.  After a long and hard struggle, Felix finally reached the other side
